    {
      "tag": "Stop 1",
      "h2": "The labour market",
      "body_html": "<p class=\"big\">The <span class=\"hl p\">labour market</span> is where employers hire workers.</p><p>It works like other markets — supply of workers meets demand from businesses.</p>"
    },
    {
      "tag": "Stop 2",
      "h2": "Sectors of work",
      "body_html": "<p>Jobs sort into sectors:</p><ul><li><b>Primary</b> — farming, mining.</li><li><b>Secondary</b> — making things in factories.</li><li><b>Tertiary</b> — services like shops, banks, NHS.</li><li><b>Quaternary</b> — tech, research.</li></ul><p>In the UK, most jobs are tertiary. 🏥</p>"
    },
    {
      "tag": "Stop 3",
      "h2": "Skilled vs unskilled",
      "body_html": "<p><span class=\"hl y\">Skilled</span> jobs need training — doctors, electricians, coders. They usually pay more.</p><p><span class=\"hl y\">Unskilled</span> jobs need less training — basic warehouse work. They pay less but offer experience.</p>"
    }
  ],
